<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=reinforced-concrete">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reinforced concrete</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ferroconcrete</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reinforced concrete is concrete with embedded iron or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> bars or mesh, which take the tension plain concrete cannot bear while the concrete resists compression. It was developed in parallel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the mid-19th century. The French builder François Coignet built the first iron-reinforced concrete structure, a four-story house in the suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in 1853–55, and in 1854 the English builder William B. Wilkinson reinforced the roof and floors of a house with knowledge of tensile stresses. Joseph Monier patented reinforced flowerpots in 1867 and columns and girders in 1877, and the firm Wayss &amp; Freytag made the first commercial use of it in 1884.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete.jpg" alt="Reinforced concrete" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lionel Allorge · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chantier_de_construction_du_complexe_associatif_multifonctions_à_Antony_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1853</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Near Paris, France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>François Coignet · William B. Wilkinson · Joseph Monier</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Coignet seems to have used the iron to keep monolithic walls from overturning rather than to add strength; Wilkinson’s reinforcement of 1854 shows knowledge of tensile stresses.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/roman-concrete">Roman concrete<span>c. 150 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/portland-cement">Portland cement<span>1824</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=reinforced-concrete">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
